WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.670 --> 00:00:01.630
- [Reporter] Orbit insertion burn

00:00:01.630 --> 00:00:04.340
was scheduled to take place
31 minutes after launch

00:00:04.340 --> 00:00:07.080
but didn't happen on schedule.

00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:08.660
- [Narrator] Boeing's Starliner spaceship

00:00:08.660 --> 00:00:11.360
was sent back to the factory in August.

00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:13.500
NASA had planned to launch the Starliner

00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:15.860
for a crewless test flight to the ISS,

00:00:15.860 --> 00:00:18.210
or International Space
Station, this past summer.

00:00:18.210 --> 00:00:21.444
But the Space Agency and
Boeing postponed the mission

00:00:21.444 --> 00:00:22.760
after a valve issue

00:00:22.760 --> 00:00:25.460
was discovered on the
vehicle before launch.

00:00:25.460 --> 00:00:27.550
- Boeing executives have
talked about this flight

00:00:27.550 --> 00:00:30.170
as one of great importance
for the company,

00:00:30.170 --> 00:00:33.630
and staff had spent months working

00:00:33.630 --> 00:00:35.730
through every issue from the first flight.

00:00:35.730 --> 00:00:37.530
- [Narrator] Challenges
with space technology

00:00:37.530 --> 00:00:39.480
or vehicles aren't uncommon.

00:00:39.480 --> 00:00:41.260
Both Boeing's Starliner project

00:00:41.260 --> 00:00:43.960
and its competitor, SpaceX's
Dragon crew capsule,

00:00:43.960 --> 00:00:45.840
have been delayed multiple times.

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:48.600
But how did Boeing, a company
with such a long history

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:50.490
at the cutting edge of the space industry

00:00:50.490 --> 00:00:52.240
suffer so many setbacks?

00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:55.827
- [Announcer] Five, four, three, two.

00:00:55.827 --> 00:00:57.080
- [Narrator] Since the
height of the Cold War,

00:00:57.080 --> 00:00:58.640
Boeing's name has been synonymous

00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:01.040
with ambitious American space endeavors.

00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:02.480
- Some of its biggest accomplishments

00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:05.920
include vehicles for the Apollo program,

00:01:05.920 --> 00:01:07.570
which sent humans to the moon.

00:01:07.570 --> 00:01:11.030
- [Narrator] In 2014, NASA
contracted two companies,

00:01:11.030 --> 00:01:14.970
SpaceX and Boeing, to create
two different space vehicles

00:01:14.970 --> 00:01:17.550
that would serve as the next
generation of spaceships

00:01:17.550 --> 00:01:19.263
to transport crew to the ISS,

00:01:20.140 --> 00:01:22.600
- SpaceX had been around for
a little more than a decade.

00:01:22.600 --> 00:01:25.750
It was still a relatively young company.

00:01:25.750 --> 00:01:28.760
Still building out its
operations and technology.

00:01:28.760 --> 00:01:33.670
Whereas Boeing had been
working with NASA for decades.

00:01:33.670 --> 00:01:36.300
- [Narrator] Since the Space
Shuttle's retirement in 2011,

00:01:36.300 --> 00:01:39.590
and until 2020, the US did
not have the capability

00:01:39.590 --> 00:01:41.692
to transport crew to orbit.

00:01:41.692 --> 00:01:42.770
(rocket blasting)
- [Announcer] And liftoff.

00:01:42.770 --> 00:01:44.020
- [Narrator] For about a decade,

00:01:44.020 --> 00:01:46.380
NASA relied on Russia's Soyuz rocket

00:01:46.380 --> 00:01:48.910
to get American crews to the ISS.

00:01:48.910 --> 00:01:52.570
- Boeing's Starliner was
designed to take astronauts

00:01:52.570 --> 00:01:54.010
to the International Space Station,

00:01:54.010 --> 00:01:55.330
and get them home.

00:01:55.330 --> 00:01:57.870
Boeing's is attached on top of a rocket,

00:01:57.870 --> 00:02:00.580
which separates after that initial launch.

00:02:00.580 --> 00:02:03.270
It's meant to travel and
dock with the Space Station.

00:02:03.270 --> 00:02:04.850
- [Announcer] And liftoff.

00:02:04.850 --> 00:02:08.910
The rise of Starliner and a
new era in human space flight.

00:02:08.910 --> 00:02:11.570
- [Narrator] In 2019,
Boeing and NASA attempted

00:02:11.570 --> 00:02:14.510
to send an uncrewed Starliner to the ISS

00:02:14.510 --> 00:02:15.680
with much fanfare.

00:02:15.680 --> 00:02:17.030
- [Announcer] Passing
one minute into flight.

00:02:17.030 --> 00:02:18.610
- [Narrator] But that
test flight failed due

00:02:18.610 --> 00:02:20.310
to two programming errors:

00:02:20.310 --> 00:02:23.250
the spacecraft's internal
timer was off by 11 hours,

00:02:23.250 --> 00:02:25.730
stranding it in the wrong
orbit, and preventing it

00:02:25.730 --> 00:02:28.380
from reaching the
International Space Station.

00:02:28.380 --> 00:02:31.500
Boeing CST-100 Starliner
capsule did, however,

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:33.250
return to Earth safely.

00:02:33.250 --> 00:02:36.470
- So we did have obviously
some challenges today.

00:02:36.470 --> 00:02:40.860
When the spacecraft separated
from the launch vehicle,

00:02:40.860 --> 00:02:43.430
we did not get the orbital insertion burn

00:02:43.430 --> 00:02:45.019
that we were hoping for.

00:02:45.019 --> 00:02:45.890
(rocket roaring)

00:02:45.890 --> 00:02:48.270
- [Narrator] Another
potentially catastrophic error

00:02:48.270 --> 00:02:50.180
was fixed during the mission.

00:02:50.180 --> 00:02:53.160
- One error sent the
Starliner to the wrong orbit,

00:02:53.160 --> 00:02:55.600
and then the second involved software

00:02:55.600 --> 00:02:58.710
that controls thrusters
that helped angle the craft

00:02:58.710 --> 00:03:00.880
to avoid damaging a heat shield,

00:03:00.880 --> 00:03:03.830
which protects the vehicle during reentry.

00:03:03.830 --> 00:03:06.600
The software emerged
after the flight failed

00:03:06.600 --> 00:03:08.450
as a sort of a major issue

00:03:08.450 --> 00:03:11.680
that Boeing needed to
address for a future attempt.

00:03:11.680 --> 00:03:13.590
- [Narrator] After the
flight, NASA's leadership

00:03:13.590 --> 00:03:16.190
was concerned that Boeing had
a broader cultural problem

00:03:16.190 --> 00:03:18.420
in light of the 737 MAX crisis,

00:03:18.420 --> 00:03:22.250
which involved two fatal crashes
in Indonesia and Ethiopia.

00:03:22.250 --> 00:03:24.950
The Agency ordered a
sweeping outside review,

00:03:24.950 --> 00:03:27.500
resulting in dozens of recommendations.

00:03:27.500 --> 00:03:31.080
- The failure of the 2019
Starliner test mission

00:03:31.080 --> 00:03:33.010
was a big deal for Boeing.

00:03:33.010 --> 00:03:35.970
Boeing was still
grappling with the fallout

00:03:35.970 --> 00:03:38.160
from the 737 MAX crisis.

00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:40.630
A successful Starliner
mission was seen as a way

00:03:40.630 --> 00:03:43.580
to help restore its reputation

00:03:43.580 --> 00:03:47.000
for reliability and engineering prowess.

00:03:47.000 --> 00:03:48.770
- [Narrator] In December 2019,

00:03:48.770 --> 00:03:51.350
Boeing's board, already
frustrated by the MAX crisis,

00:03:51.350 --> 00:03:54.010
ousted Boeing CEO Dennis Muilenburg.

00:03:54.010 --> 00:03:56.630
Then in August, Boeing
and the Space Agency said

00:03:56.630 --> 00:03:59.260
they would again postpone
the redo of the test mission

00:03:59.260 --> 00:04:01.860
because some valves on the
Starliner's propulsion system

00:04:01.860 --> 00:04:04.150
became stuck before launch.

00:04:04.150 --> 00:04:06.380
NASA declined to comment last week.

00:04:06.380 --> 00:04:08.230
The Agency said earlier in October

00:04:08.230 --> 00:04:10.340
that it and Boeing were making progress,

00:04:10.340 --> 00:04:12.570
investigating the Starliner's valves.

00:04:12.570 --> 00:04:14.780
In a statement, Boeing
said, "It is focused

00:04:14.780 --> 00:04:16.360
on supporting our NASA customer,

00:04:16.360 --> 00:04:18.840
and the path forward
will be driven by safety,

00:04:18.840 --> 00:04:20.040
and thorough testing.

00:04:20.040 --> 00:04:22.040
We have full confidence in our ability

00:04:22.040 --> 00:04:24.690
to deliver Starliner for
its ultimate mission."

00:04:24.690 --> 00:04:27.510
NASA said the vehicle won't
launch again this year.

00:04:27.510 --> 00:04:30.150
The Space Agency also
shifted two astronauts

00:04:30.150 --> 00:04:32.510
who had been assigned to
fly on Starliner missions

00:04:32.510 --> 00:04:34.410
to a future SpaceX flight.

00:04:34.410 --> 00:04:37.540
- NASA said it was
shifting these astronauts

00:04:37.540 --> 00:04:39.240
because it needed to ensure

00:04:39.240 --> 00:04:42.330
that those astronauts were
getting space experience,

00:04:42.330 --> 00:04:44.350
and it wanted to give Boeing time

00:04:44.350 --> 00:04:46.610
to develop the Starliner.

00:04:46.610 --> 00:04:49.610
- [Narrator] These most
recent Starliner setbacks come

00:04:49.610 --> 00:04:52.450
at a challenging time
for Boeing's business.

00:04:52.450 --> 00:04:55.520
After making record revenue in 2018,

00:04:55.520 --> 00:04:57.400
Boeing has lost nearly half that amount,

00:04:57.400 --> 00:04:58.910
largely due to a sharp drop

00:04:58.910 --> 00:05:00.490
in commercial aircraft deliveries

00:05:00.490 --> 00:05:02.470
and MAX-related charges.

00:05:02.470 --> 00:05:04.280
Today the company is more aligned

00:05:04.280 --> 00:05:06.630
on its space business
than it was in the past.

00:05:06.630 --> 00:05:08.680
Boeing's space program is a key part

00:05:08.680 --> 00:05:11.790
of its defense, space and security unit.

00:05:11.790 --> 00:05:15.610
- Boeing generated more
than six billion in revenue

00:05:15.610 --> 00:05:18.690
from space-related work last year,

00:05:18.690 --> 00:05:21.700
and that accounted for
a little more than 10%

00:05:21.700 --> 00:05:25.010
of all of the company's
revenue for that year.

00:05:25.010 --> 00:05:27.010
- [Narrator] Today, Boeing's
Starliner development

00:05:27.010 --> 00:05:29.590
has cost $3.2 billion,

00:05:29.590 --> 00:05:34.260
while SpaceX's Dragon has
cost far less at 2.6 billion.

00:05:34.260 --> 00:05:35.900
What's more, unlike Boeing,

00:05:35.900 --> 00:05:39.060
SpaceX has managed to get
crewed missions into space.

00:05:39.060 --> 00:05:40.390
- [Announcer] There's the
crew on the right-hand side

00:05:40.390 --> 00:05:41.223
of your screen.

00:05:41.223 --> 00:05:43.038
I think I see some more thumbs up there.

00:05:43.038 --> 00:05:48.038
(announcer chuckling)
(dramatic music)

